          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            %em possible world semantics
            one means the interpretation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"} possibility
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity"} necessity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            of “possible worlds”. Such a model is sometimes referred to as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kripke+frame"} Kripke frame
          %p
            For formalization of this in terms of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrines"} hyperdoctrines
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/necessity+and+possibility#InFirstOrderLogicAndTypeTheory"} necessity and possibility – In first-order logic and dependent type theory
          %p
            Under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            such a context interprets as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            whose interpretation is similar to that of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+space"} probability space
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measure+theory"} measure theory
            – which is one sensible formalization of possible worlds (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#TorontoMcCarthy10"} Toronto-McCarthy 10, slide 23
